Found 2026-09-27 while checking 68 recorded agent bash commands in one process: from call 37 on every check failed with 'copy failed for .../tree-sitter-bash/src/parser.c to /tmp/text-treesitter-bash-XXXX/src/parser.c: No space left on device' (Bash.pm:334); /tmp is a 3.9G tmpfs shared by the box.
Cause: Security::Checker::check_source (Checker.pm:249) does Text::Treesitter::Bash->new per call; each instance runs _build_runtime_lang_dir (Bash.pm:313), copying the grammar into a new tempdir(CLEANUP => 1) and compiling the .so with Text::Treesitter::Language::build. Nothing is shared, so each check is a C compile, and the dirs are only removed at process exit.
Reproduce: perl -Ilib -MText::Treesitter::Bash::Security::Checker -e 'my $c = Text::Treesitter::Bash::Security::Checker->new(rules => [qw(DangerousFlags)]); $c->check_source(q{ls -la}) for 1..80'
Done when: the grammar is built once per process (or cached on disk keyed by grammar version) and reused across instances and check_source calls; a test runs check_source many times in one process and asserts a single build / no new tempdirs.

2026-09-27 06:15 Reproduced on unfixed code (worktree k6-grammar-once, TMPDIR=private dir, Language::build wrapped with a counter): 4 check_source calls -> builds=1,2,3,4 and tempdirs=1,2,3,4, +13 MB per call; all removed only at process exit. Plan: cache the runtime lang dir per process in Bash.pm (file-scoped lexical, CLEANUP => 1 kept), explicit lang_dir untouched; re-validate cached dir with -d so a forked child whose parent already cleaned up rebuilds instead of failing.

